{# ==========================================================================
     05 · Speed
     The two charts and the tables under them are measured, from
     github.com/phplrt/benchmarks — absolute values, in ms and mb.

     The "how much work do you need?" panel is deliberately NOT absolute: it
     is three percentages of a full parse, and its caveat box says so. If you
     ever put microseconds back in there, put the caveat back too.
     ====================================================================== #}
<section class="band band--sunk" id="speed">
    <div class="hold">
        <div class="head rise">
            <span class="chapter">05 · speed</span>
            <h2 class="title">Ten times the input, ten times the time</h2>
            <p class="lede">
                Under a kilobyte every one of these reads a type expression in about a
                millisecond and the difference between them is noise. It is the hundredth
                kilobyte that separates them: phplrt costs ×8.8 for every ×10 of input,
                which is as close to linear as reading gets.
                <code>phpstan/phpdoc-parser</code> costs ×78 — and at a quarter of a
                megabyte that is 24 seconds against 194 ms.
            </p>
        </div>

        {#
          COORDINATE PLAN — shared by both charts on this page.

          Grid (1000 × 500). Plot box x 96…800, y 30…430.

            x = 96 + (log10(KB) + 1.60206) × 176
                25 B  = 96     250 B = 272    1 KB  = 378
                10 KB = 554    100 KB = 730   250 KB = 800

          The 5 samples span exactly four decades (0.025 KB → 250 KB), so the
          axis is a true log axis and the ticks land where the data does. The
          last two sit closer together because 100 → 250 KB really is only ×2.5.

          Time, five decades over 400px (0.5 ms … 50 000 ms):
            y = 430 − (log10(ms) + 0.30103) × 80
                1 ms = 405.9   10 = 325.9   100 = 245.9   1 s = 165.9   10 s = 85.9

          Memory, three decades over 400px (0.5 mb … 500 mb):
            y = 430 − (log10(mb) + 0.30103) × 133.333
                1 mb = 389.9   10 mb = 256.5   100 mb = 123.2

          Every point below is that formula applied to the table in the same
          panel; nothing here is eyeballed.
        #}
